<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.5.3: Distance as a Quadratic Function of Elapsed Time</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01816</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.5.3: Distance as a Quadratic Function of Elapsed Time</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ee7d047c-b84c-4e5f-8557-c05b27773ca0</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><para id="para-00001">Galileo Galilei, an Italian scientist, and other medieval scholars studied the motion of free-falling objects. The
  law they discovered can be expressed by the equation \(d=16t^2\), which gives the distance fallen in feet as a
  function of time, \(t\), in seconds.</para>
<para id="para-00002">An object is dropped from a height of 576 feet.</para>
<section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002"><title>Activity</title><exercise id="_482c9c67-5fb3-42e8-9dfa-474eb2895614" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00001"><para id="para-00003"/><para id="para-00004">How many feet does it fall in 0.5 seconds?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00005">Correct! It falls 4 feet in 0.5 seconds because \(16 (0.5)^2=4\).</para><para id="para-00006">The correct answer is 4. It falls 4 feet in 0.5 seconds because \(16 (0.5)^2=4\).</para></solution></exercise></section><list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
  <item>To find out where the object is the first few seconds after it was dropped, Elena and Diego created different
    tables.</item>
</list>
<para id="para-00007">Elena’s table:</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00001">
  <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        Time (Seconds)
     </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        Distance Fallen (Feet)
     </emphasis></entry></row>
  <row>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        16
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        64
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(t\)
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>

<para id="para-00008">Diego’s table:</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00002">
  <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        Time (Seconds)
     </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        Distance from the Ground (Feet)
     </emphasis></entry></row>
  <row>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
      <entry>
        576
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        560
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        512
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        t
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem id="prob-00002">
    <para id="para-00009"> How are the two tables alike?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00002">
    
    <para id="para-00010"/>
  
  <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00012">They both have time in seconds and distance in feet as the two quantities being measured. They both show
      0–4 seconds and \(t\) seconds for time. They describe the same object falling.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem id="prob-00003">
    <para id="para-00013"> How are the two tables different?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00003">
    
    <para id="para-00014"/>
  
  <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00016">Elena’s table measures distance fallen, and Diego’s table measures distance from the ground. They
      record measurements from opposite ends. Elena’s table starts with 0 feet for 0 seconds, and Diego’s
      starts with 576 feet for 0 seconds.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
  <item>Complete Elena’s table. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003"><exercise id="_07cfb666-b9a1-4cc5-b6a4-78e440bd664e" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00004"><para id="para-00017"/><para id="para-00018"> What is the distance the object has fallen 3 seconds after it was dropped?</para>
        </problem><solution id="sol-00004"><para id="para-00019">Correct! 144. The object has fallen 144 feet 3 seconds after it was dropped.</para><para id="para-00020">The correct answer is 144. The object has fallen 144 feet 3 seconds after it was dropped.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_9cb0e6c1-2f5c-4b7f-b400-addd69c11b52" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00005"><para id="para-00021"/><para id="para-00022"> What is the distance the object has fallen 4 seconds after it was dropped?</para>
        </problem><solution id="sol-00005"><para id="para-00023">Correct! 256. The object has fallen 256 feet 4 seconds after it was dropped.</para><para id="para-00024">The correct answer is 256. The object has fallen 256 feet 4 seconds after it was dropped.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<example id="exam-00001"><exercise>
  <problem id="prob-00006">
    
    <para id="para-00025"> What is the distance the object has fallen \(t\) seconds after it was dropped?</para>
      <para id="para-00026">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00006">
  <para id="para-00027">Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00028">\(16t^2\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00003">
  <item>Complete Diego’s table. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00004"><exercise id="f680c9cc-d48d-4627-921e-1837b60ec9f1" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00007"><para id="para-00029"/><para id="para-00030"> What is the object’s distance from the ground 3 seconds after it was dropped?</para>
        </problem><solution id="sol-00007"><para id="para-00031">Correct! 432. The object is 432 feet from the ground 3 seconds after it was dropped.</para><para id="para-00032">The correct answer is 432. The object is 432 feet from the ground 3 seconds after it was dropped.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="fea13522-15ea-4820-b214-42f58d143dad" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00008"><para id="para-00033"/><para id="para-00034"> What is the object’s distance from the ground 4 seconds after it was dropped?</para>
        </problem><solution id="sol-00008"><para id="para-00035">Correct! 320. The object is 320 feet from the ground 4 seconds after it was dropped.</para><para id="para-00036">The correct answer is 320. The object is 320 feet from the ground 4 seconds after it was dropped.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<example id="exam-00002"><exercise>
  <problem id="prob-00009">
    
    <para id="para-00037"> What is the object’s distance from the ground \(t\) seconds after it was dropped?</para>
      <para id="para-00038">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00009">
  <para id="para-00039">Compare your Answer:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00040">\(576-16t^2\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>

